WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.261
A flame survives only while it makes heat faster than it loses it.

2
00:00:04.644 --> 00:00:07.918
Lower the oxygen, and the flame makes less heat.

3
00:00:08.359 --> 00:00:12.910
At some point the losses win, and the flame goes out on its own.

4
00:00:13.421 --> 00:00:23.243
The oxygen level where that happens is called the flammability limit. For choosing cabin materials, it may be the most useful number there is.

5
00:00:23.847 --> 00:00:30.070
NASA's FLEX experiment burned hundreds of fuel droplets at different oxygen levels.

6
00:00:30.674 --> 00:00:37.930
Below fifteen percent oxygen, twenty seven of twenty eight droplet flames went out on their own.

7
00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:47.624
Between thirty and fifty percent, only two of twenty four did. Four kept burning, and the rest were not recorded.

8
00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:56.750
FLEX changed other things too, like the fuel and the pressure, so this is a pattern, not a controlled test of oxygen alone.

9
00:00:57.075 --> 00:00:58.875
But the direction is clear.

10
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:06.920
More oxygen means flames that are harder to stop: the fire risk side of every high oxygen habitat.
